package Main;

import Core.Output;
import Core.FileSystemModule;
import Core.FileSystemModule.FileSystem;
import Core.FileSystemModule.FileSystemError;
import Core.Runtime.Entry;
import Core.Runtime.EntryKind;

// Scoped advisory file locking (DEC-348) — `FileSystem.withLock(path, fn)`.
//
// The lock is taken before `fn` runs and released when the block exits, on EVERY path: a normal
// return, a `return` out of the closure, or a throw. There is no `lock()`/`unlock()` pair to
// mis-pair, which is the point — the ruling rejected a manual API precisely because that is the shape
// every language regrets. Internally `withLock` is a `using (FileLock …)` block (DEC-364), so the
// release guarantee is the scope guard's, not a promise this function makes separately.
//
// WHOLE-FILE and ADVISORY. It excludes other `withLock` holders and anything else using the same OS
// lock — notably PHP's `flock()`, so a phorj program and the PHP it transpiles to contend for the
// SAME lock. It does NOT stop an unrelated program from reading or writing the file.
//
// Byte-range locking and timeouts were both rejected by the ruling: byte-range needs `fcntl`, and a
// timeout would need a spin-sleep, which is a bandaid rather than a mechanism.
//
// `tryWithLock` is the NON-BLOCKING sibling: it returns `Option<T>` — `None` when the lock is held by
// someone else, `Some(v)` when the closure ran and returned `v`. It returns `Option` rather than the
// cheaper `T?` because with `T?` a busy lock and a closure that legitimately returned null would be
// the SAME value, and that ambiguity type-checks clean (developer-ruled 2026-07-31). Note the example
// below needs no `import Core.Option` — the FS prelude imports it, and prelude imports are honoured
// transitively.
//
// [Unverified on Windows] Verified on Linux only, where `/proc/locks` shows `FLOCK ADVISORY` and a
// Rust holder and a PHP `flock()` holder block each other in both directions. Windows is a shipped
// target, its lock semantics may be MANDATORY rather than advisory, and there is no Windows CI — so
// treat the cross-platform behaviour as unverified.
// CONCURRENCY-SAFETY OF THIS EXAMPLE ITSELF (worth reading — it is the feature eating its own cooking).
// The repo's test corpus runs every example CONCURRENTLY (`tests/format.rs` fans the corpus across
// cores while `tests/differential.rs` runs it too), and phorj has no per-process unique-path source, so
// a shipped example that mutates a FIXED temp path can be running several times at once. The earlier
// shape of this file was racy for that reason: it began by DELETING its working directory, so one run
// could remove the counter another run was mid-way through tallying, and print a different number.
//
// The fix is the subject of the example: everything mutating runs inside `withLock(serial)`, so
// concurrent runs SERIALIZE and each produces identical output. No `removeDirAll`, and no directory at
// all — the paths sit directly in the temp dir, which always exists. State is reset by WRITING the
// counter empty under the lock rather than by deleting anything.
//
// The inner demos use a DIFFERENT lock file (`guard`) than the outer `serial` one on purpose: the OS
// lock is per-file-descriptor, so a blocking `withLock` nested on the SAME path would deadlock against
// itself. That is the same property step 4 below turns into a deterministic demonstration.
// A tally file bumped under the lock — the canonical read-modify-write, where the danger is another
// holder interleaving BETWEEN the modify and the read-back. One `x` is appended per bump and the new
// count is the file's size.
function bump(string counter): int throws FileSystemError {
    return FileSystem.withLock(
        counter,
        function(): int throws FileSystemError { FileSystem.appendText(counter, "x")?; return FileSystem.size(counter)?; }
    )?;
}

#[Entry(kind: EntryKind.Cli)]
function main(): void {
    try {
        string tmp = FileSystem.tempDir();
        string serial = "{tmp}/phorj-lock-example-serial.lock";
        string counter = "{tmp}/phorj-lock-example-counter.txt";
        string guard = "{tmp}/phorj-lock-example-guard.lock";
        // Everything below is inside ONE lock, which is what makes the output identical no matter how
        // many copies of this example run at once.
        discard FileSystem.withLock(
            serial,
            function(): int throws FileSystemError { FileSystem.writeText(counter, "")?; Output.printLine("bump -> {bump(counter)?}"); Output.printLine("bump -> {bump(counter)?}"); int a = FileSystem.withLock(guard, function(): int throws FileSystemError { return 1; })?; int b = FileSystem.withLock(guard, function(): int throws FileSystemError { return 2; })?; Output.printLine("sequential acquisitions: {a} then {b}"); try { discard FileSystem.withLock(guard, function(): int throws FileSystemError { discard FileSystem.readText("{tmp}/phorj-lock-example-absent")?; return 0; }); Output.printLine("unreachable"); } catch (FileSystemError e) { Output.printLine("closure threw, and it propagated"); } int after = FileSystem.withLock(guard, function(): int throws FileSystemError { return 3; })?; Output.printLine("lock was free after the throw: {after}"); string nested = FileSystem.withLock(guard, function(): string throws FileSystemError { Option<string> inner = FileSystem.tryWithLock(guard, function(): string throws FileSystemError { return "inner acquired"; })?; return match (inner) { Option.Some(v) => v, Option.None() => "tryWithLock saw it held" }; })?; Output.printLine(nested); Option<string> free = FileSystem.tryWithLock(guard, function(): string throws FileSystemError { return "acquired"; })?; Output.printLine(match (free) { Option.Some(v) => "tryWithLock on a free lock: {v}", Option.None() => "unreachable" }); return 0; }
        );
    } catch (FileSystemError e) {
        // Reset by CONTENT, not by deleting the tree — a delete is what made this racy before.
        // 1. The closure's value comes back out of `withLock`.
        // 2. Re-acquiring in the same program only works because the block released. A leaked lock
        //    would deadlock here rather than print.
        // 3. A closure that THROWS still releases on the way out — the error propagates unchanged,
        //    and the next acquisition succeeds.
        // 4. `tryWithLock` reports contention instead of waiting. DETERMINISTIC with no second
        //    process and no sleep: the OS lock is per-file-DESCRIPTOR, so the inner attempt opens
        //    its own descriptor and genuinely finds the lock held — by this very program.
        // 5. …and on a FREE lock the same call acquires and hands back the closure's value. `guard`
        //    is free here: every block above released it, and a concurrent run cannot hold it
        //    because it is still waiting on `serial`.
        Output.printLine("unexpected: {e.message}");
    }
}
